Chezz <steve@cheseborough.com> wrote:
> I have been playing the game for a couple years. Have not won it, but
> I get down to the basement regularly.
>      In an effort to improve my play, I have been reading some posts
> on this list. And one long one, by a player who seems way beyond my
> level, puzzles me. He advises avoiding combat, and letting the pet
> take care of most of the monsters. Apparently he enjoys great success
> through this style.

The plus side of letting your pet kill a lot of monsters is that you
stay low level (thus limiting the nasty monsters) and tend to collect
more 'loot'.  Great for weaker classes.

>      What puzzles me is that even playing basically the opposite of
> that approach -- that is, clobbering almost everything in sight -- I
> often get to the Quest level with my character at too low an
> experience level to qualify for the Quest. Or just barely qualifying
> for it, which I have found is not as good as being a level or two
> higher than that, if you want to survive the Quest.

I often arrive at the quest at too low of a level to do it.  I tend to
find a good altar nearby and quietly build up levels and collect things.
Many people tend to skip the quest and do the castle first for the wand.
However, for classes with decent quest artifacts (like the Valk) the
quest is useful to do first.

>      So, if you play the combat-avoidance style, how do move up the
> levels? Do you just skip the Quest and go on? Or do you catch up on
> all of them just before the Quest, by going back upstairs and then
> clobbering everything in sight? Or is there some other method that I'm
> not seeing here? Thanks.

I think your main problem is moving too fast.  Wait.  Learn how not to
starve to death.  Sit around on the same level for awhile.

For playing style 'tricks' there is at least one public nethack server
that you can log into and watch other people play.  Rather useful if
pick a good player and observe their 'tricks'.

